  "live_records": {
    "q-39856": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39856",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle.",
